arch note
name On Compound Operations and the Causes of Valleynoy?s Collapse
msg
On Compound Operations and the Causes of Valleynoy?s Collapse

Restricted archive. Damage suggests deliberate concealment.

This document records procedures that were discontinued shortly before the fall of Valleynoy. The author does not recommend their continuation.

1. Compound Alteration

It is possible to attempt the simultaneous negotiation of properties between multiple objects. This process differs fundamentally from standard alteration and requires all objects to be present within the container.

The operation does not improve objects individually. Instead, it attempts to reconcile their properties into a shared configuration.

Success rates were low.

2. Instability and Propagation

Compound operations introduce instability that propagates through all involved objects. Failure affects every component simultaneously. Recovery is not possible once the catalyst is introduced.

Several artifacts created during this period exhibited unpredictable behavior even after apparent success.

3. Container Memory

Repeated high-intensity operations permanently alter the container itself. Containers exposed to multiple failures demonstrate increased volatility.

It was standard practice, late in Valleynoy, to destroy containers after catastrophic attempts. This practice began too late.

4. Summary of Findings

Artificial alteration is viable within controlled bounds. Beyond those bounds, outcomes become increasingly erratic. Valleynoy?s final years were characterized by escalating attempts to bypass established limits.

The city was not destroyed by lack of knowledge, but by deliberate disregard of accumulated data.
endmsg
end
end
