msg
Foundational Procedures for Directed Item Alteration
 Ink composition suggests pre-collapse Valleynoy.

This document records the minimum repeatable procedure for altering an existing object through artificial means. The method described herein has been reproduced successfully under controlled conditions no fewer than thirty-seven times. Deviations from these steps are not recommended.

1. Selection of the Target Object

Only one object is to be altered per procedure. The object must be physically intact and capable of bearing stress. Objects already bearing complex enchantments exhibit reduced responsiveness. Items with no prior alteration history show the most predictable outcomes.

Empirical note: Items that have seen prolonged use but little repair respond better than pristine ceremonial items.

The object shall be referred to as the target for the remainder of this document.

2. Preparation of the Container

The operation must occur within a bounded vessel: forge basin, cauldron, or sealed crucible. Open surfaces produce inconsistent results.

The container must be empty and cleaned of residue from previous attempts. Residual material from failed operations has been observed to interfere with subsequent attempts, occasionally biasing outcomes toward unintended attributes.

Once preparation begins, the container must not be moved.

3. Required Components

Three component categories are mandatory:

A liquid catalyst (commonly prepared as a potion).

One inorganic material.

One portion of organic tissue.

The catalyst must be introduced last. Introducing it earlier has resulted in premature reaction and loss of materials.

The inorganic material determines the nature of the alteration. Experiments confirm that the material?s inherent properties correlate strongly with the resulting modification. The process does not allow for multiple properties to be targeted simultaneously.

The organic component serves as a binding agent. Substitutes have been tested and universally failed. Without it, alterations either do not persist or degrade rapidly.

4. Execution

Place the target object in the container first. Add the inorganic material second. Add the organic component third. Confirm all materials are present before introducing the catalyst.

Once the catalyst is introduced, the container should be sealed or otherwise left undisturbed. Visual changes typically manifest within seconds. The absence of visible reaction does not indicate failure.

5. Outcome and Risk

Successful alteration results in a permanent enhancement to one characteristic of the target object. The magnitude of improvement correlates with the difficulty of the attempt.

Failures consume materials. In rare cases, the target object suffers degradation. Destruction has been observed only when attempting alterations beyond established thresholds.

Repeated alterations increase resistance. This resistance appears cumulative.
endmsg
end
end
